n/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/2d/2db3e139bdff0aabdbe352d83e1b4ffc7dbdaf3c01dd070343778910b10384ea.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "733eab11-2a26-5402-8df5-bba50c9beaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f43fbb-95b7-541f-8b6c-884210699c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400752} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6022ff-41cc-5e6f-94c9-3fc31db2cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,14,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400753} {:answer/id #uuid "45ff73b4-62a8-5ce3-88c9-d43c398f4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-10,2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400754} {:answer/id #uuid "904fd9b0-d482-5093-afb0-55078a667399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-14,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400755} {:answer/id #uuid "f884e2c3-8624-5ca1-87bf-175fa491b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f43fbb-95b7-541f-8b6c-884210699c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400752} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f171d5e-1ede-583a-b2ab-a54f8c858c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ea4789-04ff-53b9-83f0-42f591cf2261" :question/math-academy-id "q-1393" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨4,5,-1⟩$ and $v=⟨-6,0,2⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-6))j+(4\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-6))k \\\\  & =(10-0)i-(8-6)j+(0-(-30))k \\\\  & =10i-2j+30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Parallelogram Spanned by Two Non-Parallel Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186ee2ac-da11-5516-b903-e3b5e9480103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266346} {:answer/id #uuid "303f5039-136f-57c4-95b6-e45427ec1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1f355e-f850-51c9-9c2a-fb7bc5bbb357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266684} {:answer/id #uuid "92a8e1d3-8eda-5c2f-9bb2-70ec28082ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267452} {:answer/id #uuid "a686bd68-cab0-5c69-85ea-bad821967fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303f5039-136f-57c4-95b6-e45427ec1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad50c151-5c0e-566c-ac0d-79838b4014d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cad6730b-4b4a-5d91-acf2-b03ce7ce4d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13944" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a70285258e584708a7296a2a008536dec9c97750b48e5fe0eb2f8cb7761bed1.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-10\\,|=10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Parallelogram Spanned by Two Non-Parallel Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d768a08-43a7-50f3-bc2c-9d0aec2d145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266547} {:answer/id #uuid "49c440bd-ae95-5742-ba82-03be224e0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} {:answer/id #uuid "650a0407-08e2-55ea-afa8-98afe8bbc506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267039} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1ac682-0ef5-54bf-9790-851d28380cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267230} {:answer/id #uuid "dba30746-1bcd-5e32-976d-2a26e890257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c440bd-ae95-5742-ba82-03be224e0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ea674a-8479-5833-a353-9cc54ecf9166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19e02ca3-a6f7-5909-838c-8df1ebb7ea40" :question/math-academy-id "q-13951" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257b433d-960b-5602-9139-f0a08f725b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269602} {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} {:answer/id #uuid "a28f67e7-b07f-5e99-99f1-6ef0ca27df71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270719} {:answer/id #uuid "ab496260-2eb5-5f24-b36a-b5c83e218fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270799} {:answer/id #uuid "cee52816-3d9a-5a2d-bf35-0841387ab28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a50b6d4-0ed8-59a9-bfe9-13d431937f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf86f867-a1a5-56dc-89e8-e2255e71399b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139689" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $a$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $b$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $c$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09aeb7f5-31e0-5b2d-9e2a-8eace54a6665" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product of Two Vectors Whose Angle Is a Non-Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60742969-581c-5973-8fa9-78e77a0bf094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270120} {:answer/id #uuid "622b545e-1af8-5ad8-944d-8f87481ebdc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270133} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbbc011-0ab5-5321-8b62-8396249479da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270362} {:answer/id #uuid "84e582c4-e0ad-504a-9f91-14756ec4390b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270417} {:answer/id #uuid "b177a505-8449-5d54-8746-4506cea92190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cbbc011-0ab5-5321-8b62-8396249479da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0ffccc-6715-58cf-bae1-c7df2b05cefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fb7d87a-bb90-5349-a9b4-58de72e66222" :question/math-academy-id "q-1400" :question/problem "The vector $a$ has magnitude $9,$ the vector $b$ has magnitude $5,$ the angle between $a$ and $b$ is ${140}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $a\\times b.$ Find $a\\times b$ in terms of $n.$ Round your final answer to the nearest integer where appropriate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|a|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =45\\cdot (0.642…)\\cdot n \\\\  & =(28.925…)\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈29n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} {:answer/id #uuid "2044f8ba-a526-5264-9c8b-279475bc6079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269538} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef60e4f-a72a-5357-acf3-4233e5a280e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-25{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1396c9b-3b30-5a34-9af3-bb9767985973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271110} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a70aa-76d8-5b9c-aa63-ecd9752ccf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} :answer-field/id #uuid "501bcbfe-9b64-5b29-8ec4-43efe59a3a60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5ade6b-92c1-5055-8b0b-c80cbaff9d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140281" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+25 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1 & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098366e9-6bbf-557f-8b0b-c28f1f5c5d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269356} {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} {:answer/id #uuid "9d389434-bcbe-5f2b-ab97-000df772d7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270659} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8a2c46-7cb2-59b0-9203-35410fcdd6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-22{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270793} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f56343-9654-51c6-8939-85100071b4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} :answer-field/id #uuid "90eb344c-2847-58a2-8769-e020f945532e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff51e775-e2b4-585e-a683-f90782853abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-140283" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6cd48df-2f97-5712-a9cd-2424a7316741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24-20\\text{i} \\\\ 10+51\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388669} {:answer/id #uuid "95fc9e31-78de-5a72-ba67-037ef90ef4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17-45\\text{i} \\\\ 16-23\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388670} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3a3835-35cc-5fe7-ac87-d525b00ee3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16-23\\text{i} \\\\ -17-45\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388671} {:answer/id #uuid "beb3530a-0d39-56a5-ac25-c46cb61ecd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10+51\\text{i} \\\\ 24-20\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388672} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4b1e6f-57a2-55e5-8684-2d11faa44fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16-27\\text{i} \\\\ 23-35\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3a3835-35cc-5fe7-ac87-d525b00ee3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16-23\\text{i} \\\\ -17-45\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388671} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0b94a0-5040-59ed-89cb-a0fb40b434bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eed0301f-d83a-548c-89d9-f113ce59919b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140329" :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-3+5\\text{i} & 4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $\\,Ax=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrix by the vector using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+5\\text{i} & 4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-3+5\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})+(4-3\\text{i})(4-5\\text{i}) \\\\ (-4\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i})(4-5\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(15+9\\text{i})+(1-32\\text{i}) \\\\ (-12)+(-5-45\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16-23\\text{i} \\\\ -17-45\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} {:answer/id #uuid "51675449-e3ed-5d32-a5a1-dc181d745b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{161}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269997} {:answer/id #uuid "64001e55-d244-54a8-918a-a1c0059b44a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} {:answer/id #uuid "88d86ace-1240-59e6-93cf-c53adca2f146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61-{(y+18)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270450} {:answer/id #uuid "e002a466-5bda-58b6-b497-ea8df55b4790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} :answer-field/id #uuid "19597342-b978-5e00-8333-b2a8755a3138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6187e130-749f-5a82-add1-d28e2491c294" :question/math-academy-id "q-140333" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+9y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+9y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+9y+20) & =-({(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2441e55c-3656-5b9c-9444-a562a3ff3b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269586} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3faa2d-7189-540c-8002-572e91da81bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269984} {:answer/id #uuid "82317f98-eb55-5a2a-84b7-c0fc441aa39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270394} {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} {:answer/id #uuid "f37d3db7-779c-579c-a14f-a3675bd5b658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} :answer-field/id #uuid "02be3ebb-ff12-5196-b096-96eac16ef254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9{z}^{2}-6z+9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9.$ So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef2e7753-83f4-5826-a04f-89616d34c578" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations to a Complex Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c963e2-b3cb-58d6-a59c-fe499469b36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}-3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269621} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8223a2-8958-5262-ac03-de7f96f33349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}-3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269828} {:answer/id #uuid "60834e42-7bac-516c-a42e-6484d29e1833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270121} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9eca61-8fbd-5971-aa68-1c3ea7a21fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270467} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b0227c-0fdf-5a87-b2b7-d09e74d53b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b0227c-0fdf-5a87-b2b7-d09e74d53b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271261} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fcada7-7fcb-5dcb-8ff2-a1a59df1b572" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3615415-e228-51f7-a06b-2ad2c7f60844" :question/math-academy-id "q-140381" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i} & \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following  elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}=\\frac{1}{1+2\\text{i}}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(2\\text{i}-3){R}_{1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i} & \\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{1+2\\text{i}}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3-2\\text{i} & \\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(2\\text{i}-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d854445-4b9d-5457-8fdd-3e9aafaf84c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269970} {:answer/id #uuid "6c18b098-7360-5163-a283-eff16352c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270231} {:answer/id #uuid "89f2f572-b7c1-5dea-a169-978a09aab668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}+{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bcef6-9c81-5bea-950e-d8b1ddb76848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270787} {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354} :answer-field/id #uuid "880bee47-a8c3-50a6-937c-697f411c6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afc8b4da-a3f1-5590-a7d5-e7a094b395bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140410" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0884ad38-9d14-5f8e-b665-d8b35b5af237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269346} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ac08-c4ce-5d3f-845f-d38358413506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269388} {:answer/id #uuid "2934301c-7684-5c1f-b910-d7819431eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269640} {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} {:answer/id #uuid "81639cc6-e903-5cd2-bc14-50bfc72b4d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c2979c-8072-5209-9ca0-20ccbaf77003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2ffa4af-5dbe-569b-8d78-f233584a8913" :question/math-academy-id "q-140431" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x}+8x)\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{1}+8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2e+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "733eab11-2a26-5402-8df5-bba50c9beaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aac8c206-b291-573d-ae2a-74259dfb0e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 28,-19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056635} {:answer/id #uuid "ca53652c-eb08-5737-b6e1-44c670d816d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 18, 19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056636} {:answer/id #uuid "f956129b-c0d4-5106-85ef-d4cae79b9a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 28, 19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056637} {:answer/id #uuid "5c983fbc-29f4-567c-af19-bcd23d5769f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨28,-19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056638} {:answer/id #uuid "69487641-8222-5353-80f9-06c01524c50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨18, 19, 61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056639}] :answer-field/id #uuid "55babe7a-df9e-5246-9069-b8e548bbfc62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0430e0c4-a0b4-5049-ad23-35a8288d7cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28,19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388752} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a43164-df74-5f59-9f11-3639b1e68c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28,-19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388753} {:answer/id #uuid "fca0458d-7adb-55af-9b20-df320360f312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨28,-19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388754} {:answer/id #uuid "afb78719-5f2d-59cd-b63d-0e3512a7ce7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-18,19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388755} {:answer/id #uuid "cb139f18-1fd5-55e0-b900-e3e088cf39d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨18,19,61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb78719-5f2d-59cd-b63d-0e3512a7ce7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-18,19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388755} :answer-field/id #uuid "26765ce1-5d1e-5d12-ad0e-0e45e31383cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7427d773-9726-5cf6-8261-06dbd4949f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1405" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-3,10,4⟩$ and $v=⟨4,7,1⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)i-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(10-28)i-(-3-16)j+(-21-40)k \\\\  & =-18i+19j-61k \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcf88a3-1e20-5a4c-bb86-1fe1bdaa1963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396054} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d6ea0-c5ed-5e7f-9687-ab98aef0c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396055} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc7a4e-aacb-5122-89ac-d5eb5d04a7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396056} {:answer/id #uuid "950ec668-d2b7-5d89-8e4f-1d349b1209a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396057} {:answer/id #uuid "2789771c-579f-5375-8680-854defcdf4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d6ea0-c5ed-5e7f-9687-ab98aef0c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396055} :answer-field/id #uuid "1984d219-413d-5333-ac39-1acd7d9d0ec5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1270303e-0109-58be-ac65-6aecbfb8540f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14050" :question/problem "![](images/04/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{7}{4}+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12857ff7-7f34-53f2-92a8-fc5552e5223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269431} {:answer/id #uuid "18c99cf2-a2ea-5550-8e35-29afed4e9f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269476} {:answer/id #uuid "555c78b8-9307-5a0d-84c0-e46552fd5cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270027} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9d61f-bacc-5381-9864-7b41873c8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} {:answer/id #uuid "c47e9ae5-65c0-542a-acd0-bd7144abd9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9d61f-bacc-5381-9864-7b41873c8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7ed00bf-fcba-544c-8d6c-e3b5291f4354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "180f854f-d44c-5145-855a-5e70b4193e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral That Gives the Area Bounded by a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed59c0fa-145a-58c6-9183-7a618b9935a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388773} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2b15db-3e26-5206-ac2f-5ed2fb6e032f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388774} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6c7690-6ae3-52da-bc3d-2f05754b1818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388775} {:answer/id #uuid "66ed0fb5-f500-5d8a-908f-f603def527e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388776} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7a63c1-cb03-5469-bed3-2e5a4974c10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2b15db-3e26-5206-ac2f-5ed2fb6e032f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388774} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b97b39-9214-5cd5-be06-dad9c9ec0e0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb68cdd3-2757-5be6-bde1-043b15f42f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-140583" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b729d243b344df6b26757773bf0521dea80da34fc727541d24babd3732563cf.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{a}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [-1,\\frac{5}{2}],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110582 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195e65d7-eb26-529e-a9d4-45e8bc648f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269480} {:answer/id #uuid "41ad119b-3f99-5798-aae3-071313ac3489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269862} {:answer/id #uuid "47b8eb5f-5809-5a9f-b0ac-610e2f2052e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269920} {:answer/id #uuid "a590499c-1376-53a0-949e-dc6b356ceac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270750} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fb2498-f4bf-5bc2-bb68-e36780b0ba17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8fb2498-f4bf-5bc2-bb68-e36780b0ba17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271173} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97e4b6d-a04e-5384-9abd-8a860b5c1c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04b86384-ff85-50f1-b5ea-5fd29d41b106" :question/math-academy-id "q-140607" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](images/21/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037e9d9d-2ea8-51f7-80d0-7ae9e55e52d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269300} {:answer/id #uuid "059dfaa3-492f-5c25-b3da-66100c23d38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269317} {:answer/id #uuid "82859c7e-3b79-5070-a299-966c30144ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270397} {:answer/id #uuid "8db4a101-cdc1-5e00-a4de-55d0f9526398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270496} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe32131-6f96-53f9-a749-e0b0817f800e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db4a101-cdc1-5e00-a4de-55d0f9526398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270496} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9bd46d8-15df-5ecd-8291-a73cf5db6030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3077fde9-1a47-5c51-a413-6712fd678f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141036" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95faf73fa837d232a67a773afdbf456f5798d0f05de23e6ce44b41cddb1881.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "997069ef-7b15-56ef-bcff-7d33803837cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270617} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef64c38-f309-5764-b73a-299fd6ba0a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270676} {:answer/id #uuid "ef94fe58-40f4-555f-ba52-e29e912d2bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271373} {:answer/id #uuid "f04cecd5-4c99-5bf6-9506-571515d5a62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271381} {:answer/id #uuid "fd73c731-3509-51ed-8367-9f0b7988f0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f04cecd5-4c99-5bf6-9506-571515d5a62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271381} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e9d378-531e-5c47-8c2e-17e56ef2ecda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3489c0dc-af49-5e9c-8486-3990dc32dfdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141040" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcb8b5dee06f59322f2f365512065857c79715be39f96a50d91cc9062991aa6e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=10{e}^{-\\theta }$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01370d24-1383-5993-99af-9eec26948899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269270} {:answer/id #uuid "27ab6000-389d-51c9-8b50-f6b291ad32aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269617} {:answer/id #uuid "7873fabe-8603-54d4-9449-920763aad695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270337} {:answer/id #uuid "79e9239e-bcb7-5488-ac30-22a04a7a0a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270347} {:answer/id #uuid "e3716a02-1092-5cf5-8bf8-255eeafbca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3716a02-1092-5cf5-8bf8-255eeafbca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271270} :answer-field/id #uuid "805597dc-c057-5e56-900a-9aee60a8f98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "175ea2b1-bc5a-5a18-84bf-7ca76c72b0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141048" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2\\theta $  at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111878c2-0699-58b0-910f-6d8630a5d5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269422} {:answer/id #uuid "35ecd903-6302-5e24-9005-6fc60cdcb52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269761} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4d7da7-cd37-5b63-8962-bc8a8217b8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269826} {:answer/id #uuid "4ffe372f-9afb-57d1-ada1-52151674d0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269991} {:answer/id #uuid "720d69bd-f50c-5523-ab48-89fef98d1ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ecd903-6302-5e24-9005-6fc60cdcb52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269761} :answer-field/id #uuid "855d0f2c-7298-54a9-942d-2316e578ed48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cfb92f2-65f8-5b47-be13-0657b1949d40" :question/math-academy-id "q-141051" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c07657992f5c4f708cd7546902c5e26206bfa41f291a68ec7b582cb18a167fe.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2)+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na-b=2-(-2)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b15d99-5f73-5d71-a0a0-49f9be9df65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373787} {:answer/id #uuid "3cefcde2-3aa5-51a2-8f0c-24c6172e631c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373788} {:answer/id #uuid "243ba24f-c088-5741-b1dc-797cc0c88bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373789} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab4b002-dd36-598f-8102-522b7e368735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373790} {:answer/id #uuid "4a73df82-8e85-5bb1-90fc-89e5be58e836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cefcde2-3aa5-51a2-8f0c-24c6172e631c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e02b7c91-76ce-53ed-b342-163951d05f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8abc10be-3eb5-5e2c-9a94-244a431b287a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141112" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-2)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{x} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{(-2)} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89027aec-d449-5dca-8830-9b39f9c5fbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398454} {:answer/id #uuid "d1591666-636c-5480-9f22-08f9eeec3613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398455} {:answer/id #uuid "b092bcb2-de91-5a2a-9a1c-b1cf1cdf0b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398456} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0a5d44-77c5-5b01-9db7-d212e6e5f858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398457} {:answer/id #uuid "408ad86d-b3e2-50fe-ba49-d6d10e8b89e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f0a5d44-77c5-5b01-9db7-d212e6e5f858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398457} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0f43ae-19bf-5c26-8ca2-6e01020ad358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e96414b2-2fe9-598e-bc15-f2c64a7b0633" :question/math-academy-id "q-141115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x) \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dcefcd7-21a2-550b-82e1-cdf9e8efcbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4230d9-c571-50ea-9f01-98df054d2c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398462} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c7e95-9d5a-515c-9902-a9d88176c6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398463} {:answer/id #uuid "89f9fe65-61ad-531c-91e5-0d74f2e05f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398464} {:answer/id #uuid "86af0317-94e9-53ec-8661-5ce6b18962d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47c7e95-9d5a-515c-9902-a9d88176c6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0c471e-3bc9-5406-b7a8-dfcd6f763630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea6ff592-81a4-59f3-84d6-c97bdc2e3a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141116" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A+0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3559bdb9-c007-500b-83db-63f2d91cd407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400911} {:answer/id #uuid "f1984a87-93e4-523e-80b4-a37c28b7dc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400912} {:answer/id #uuid "3f15fef9-83c3-50e8-980b-72c67ab596d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400913} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c0001-2df6-56e0-ae3c-2dca7d79a58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400914} {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806f142-c711-5da8-b1d4-68c8001a3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab70a1a-e04b-524f-9866-e86f88144126" :question/math-academy-id "q-141140" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b362831ba2248e03537551c412ae7537646dcd09f0d00a598905f0d52a258da9.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is a *simple* root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a762869-8c41-5b99-945a-904933273d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270354} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0792d1-d0d7-58d4-acd0-2928fe61d064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270498} {:answer/id #uuid "a19bd95b-bbd6-55a2-9a46-010b458abb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270704} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b75751-2d73-5678-8386-167ce0d8f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270997} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb90200a-e2bb-5eaf-ba24-af1df789d82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86545112-71e2-5627-8c9c-5a320524ae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-141144" :question/problem "![](images/40/4092c0852a573a0add69c6fb7b55f393d0767a5b6a2282a10194ea27cb1be9c4.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083343 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d02aa-4d18-5048-aa21-130459dda944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{b}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269309} {:answer/id #uuid "04c41683-f0dc-54bc-9178-b3545b74139a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269312} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3eda0a-677c-5aa3-b888-727ab23907ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270366} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf85a4-4305-5caf-9564-25e503f90596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270836} {:answer/id #uuid "d203f816-3637-5cbe-b65a-060ff7ec309a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3eda0a-677c-5aa3-b888-727ab23907ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270366} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee1b032-c2c2-5b47-8356-8c8090bd7091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a18bf49-a00f-501d-8365-1640e7596df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141181" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {2}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {2}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c694a7cb-1273-5aef-bab1-b00405cb06a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5<x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400918} {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400919} {:answer/id #uuid "ee071af0-120e-5c4a-b72d-0c54e26d3687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400920} {:answer/id #uuid "395b066b-730c-5a11-889e-cc63987fd8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or    $x>5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400921} {:answer/id #uuid "3271e9c8-ddaf-5be7-852f-5b3fd22e072b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400919} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3297c0-8b61-583d-8e5e-b17352fc3208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66cebf22-0b82-5cf0-a87d-5c8eaa25290f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141187" :question/problem "![](images/46/46edcf2265f41b8fac7931802875e1b9849006834b3509a186ffc7acb510959f.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(-x-5)(x+1)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(-x-5)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=(-x-5)(x+1)\n$$\n\n  \n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e830af773829b1712c6a87955e74411b6ad9921ae1aea58029ca968ab354c0f1.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or    $x>-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69e91ce7-259a-5a11-bb79-7e6ad7ea86d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12266a51-a7c9-5483-94da-be9664d1b481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269429} {:answer/id #uuid "18eff7de-8c89-5305-88dc-9781c493dbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269477} {:answer/id #uuid "9784d4b6-191f-5d3c-bfd0-0ff7a24f4c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270602} {:answer/id #uuid "b5aefcfb-4daa-5443-aaa4-83730b1b02a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270882} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d99ef5-ddef-5389-82d4-95a6ce517307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9784d4b6-191f-5d3c-bfd0-0ff7a24f4c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270602} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f40e9a-9d94-55f1-89bf-5ae1a4030b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6e1f817-7c7e-51c8-bd09-e888759f9be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141275" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={120}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69e91ce7-259a-5a11-bb79-7e6ad7ea86d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012c6c54-c102-5db4-8d24-caf1dec68123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269269} {:answer/id #uuid "55dd97d3-3e02-5362-84db-9281bfaf98c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270029} {:answer/id #uuid "60c3ef61-d3ea-5891-85a9-bc3230333d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270123} {:answer/id #uuid "7837e062-26e0-54eb-9c88-937b73a56868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270334} {:answer/id #uuid "9974f52a-a24a-58ca-913f-99e237c9a5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9974f52a-a24a-58ca-913f-99e237c9a5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea83195-85b8-5a62-ae80-a9df9097b16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3a924d-681c-501c-bf69-7fbcdc5b6418" :question/math-academy-id "q-141278" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={180}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{180}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8bceb-5536-5271-8827-e5da61eb6060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269387} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef50584-86f1-5b31-a1a6-a37093aa3120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269846} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbd17c-de86-5394-814e-0ac710c28aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269872} {:answer/id #uuid "ced45233-a48f-5d9c-bc03-580cf170e7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271083} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ebddae-329f-55c0-89d1-c5bb4c99e094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef50584-86f1-5b31-a1a6-a37093aa3120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269846} :answer-field/id #uuid "e714385a-3268-5f76-bbf9-458125ace391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78f7a073-b897-5930-bd54-7d5e600e42be" :question/math-academy-id "q-141284" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} {:answer/id #uuid "5e69372e-e868-57ad-81a9-235542604c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400926} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3cf5dc-1a5e-5ca5-bb04-89cb5648d292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400927} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c2df-fdff-5c81-86c1-ed79a5590ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400928} {:answer/id #uuid "ee524a7c-b00b-54ff-b0db-1379d3f0eb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83cb541-9ba7-558c-804e-5b78b4a327b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" :question/math-academy-id "q-141310" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30847f0b-0b4b-5f36-a3b0-37f3dad54553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269706} {:answer/id #uuid "545f1118-43f6-5a00-8098-f3be2988683a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270021} {:answer/id #uuid "578fba5d-47fa-5591-9315-034b97234804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270042} {:answer/id #uuid "78b7a0e1-7f04-5430-9f17-d26da04c9f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{11}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270339} {:answer/id #uuid "edad3d6a-2c87-516e-bac4-fb514407d70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578fba5d-47fa-5591-9315-034b97234804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270042} :answer-field/id #uuid "f78976ba-707b-5d42-8224-1405db058378" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "385046ae-9549-5d03-b987-27cdd162ac0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141314" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{11}{9})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{11}{9}.$ \n\nSince $|r|>1,$ the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e082063-0bec-52c0-9abb-ccfd65629060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269835} {:answer/id #uuid "73fdf72c-3fcc-55d7-b59a-2a42f091f341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270293} {:answer/id #uuid "97e6d079-ff4a-5073-8f18-1ff573cc25fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270606} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd06048-2d47-53ef-85a1-abd7a31133d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271349} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1b5d28-56a1-52d2-aca2-940b6a405673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff1b5d28-56a1-52d2-aca2-940b6a405673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271499} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bbb705-bc82-52a8-990a-0598294a9982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cba8d40e-9ff0-576c-b4c2-5a73d8552590" :question/math-academy-id "q-141322" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThis given sequence is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges if \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145b8ac2-e119-5bb5-97b4-57b7f6c36df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269448} {:answer/id #uuid "23b49514-142c-5ec7-b618-4ff74f034103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269582} {:answer/id #uuid "83d2c3b6-5dd1-5ee8-9fca-f3e5a69a70d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270407} {:answer/id #uuid "c10a13ae-f2e1-5b81-ac68-d491a279c9dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270980} {:answer/id #uuid "cfef26a9-ce94-5d52-8b32-42f376028599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "145b8ac2-e119-5bb5-97b4-57b7f6c36df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269448} :answer-field/id #uuid "be528d01-eb38-5742-aef2-18fc25b51ab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "342f5e64-a399-5f8b-a691-0fd529d174f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141323" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{4}{(\\frac{8}{x})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{8}{x}.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{8}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c76b1f7-d307-5022-b523-2a9d701df0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269821} {:answer/id #uuid "458f17d5-ae3c-5206-88da-0f8f9e8c40ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269904} {:answer/id #uuid "95ddf996-8bf3-5861-ba6b-942a4fee4bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270585} {:answer/id #uuid "b185b23a-d4dd-5aaa-ab15-5d137417fe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270856} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b0195a-a623-51e6-b397-aa476e1870d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c76b1f7-d307-5022-b523-2a9d701df0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269821} :answer-field/id #uuid "9037fd3f-bf8c-5555-9427-c1f893b2831d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df355f69-884b-5a24-91f1-5877dbb36e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141332" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{2}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{2}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25783b4f-f3f1-5cc2-84df-24e666b31d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269600} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffdb6d7-6d98-5836-9d05-4da7a12f0aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270259} {:answer/id #uuid "84074aaa-f06b-558f-bcf0-aa133aa7738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270409} {:answer/id #uuid "c08bd8f7-a322-5e2b-8ce7-5315830bd7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270974} {:answer/id #uuid "fc967a21-6b7e-51a5-8070-4d6aaad3f36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84074aaa-f06b-558f-bcf0-aa133aa7738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270409} :answer-field/id #uuid "4424e579-c28d-53af-bbb7-f1ff4f62f6c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5eb1983-1627-51b5-a21b-a1c11d629a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141364" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={30}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58fed7da-cc9f-5247-8790-595e7762d159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270054} {:answer/id #uuid "73b132a3-1b01-5bd1-b120-eb314773c1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270291} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f46a2-7866-51fb-a0a2-148a048c473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270673} {:answer/id #uuid "b706b99b-e2bf-5192-9d1b-0550db1e6cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270890} {:answer/id #uuid "d26b41fd-cb0c-55ab-b391-1f87a7808d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f46a2-7866-51fb-a0a2-148a048c473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270673} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c41cbbf-00d6-55e7-a081-545195af6cce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48b60017-29fb-5b57-99c2-0a9425be35c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141365" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={60}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aff4cbe-6fe4-5b7e-818d-a180abe9eb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269488} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebb1f5f-41ff-5401-ad84-cc41307a79d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269527} {:answer/id #uuid "38362095-3b1c-5bfd-9cc4-30473fe3b5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269776} {:answer/id #uuid "4003a8ec-c9e7-5741-ba8f-90325c814912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269851} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19c52e-9497-565a-b200-4435179ca96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4003a8ec-c9e7-5741-ba8f-90325c814912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269851} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc5eb38-7a7e-586b-9f31-c5ca7c948e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c98a2195-4ec8-5e2a-a4f5-76fb1079c255" :question/math-academy-id "q-141374" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} {:answer/id #uuid "2342b124-c9e0-58c0-a580-8d4f8695808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269577} {:answer/id #uuid "69b15b7b-0cac-5374-93f1-818c8990768a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270207} {:answer/id #uuid "a358caaa-5c57-5746-b54c-5548bfb79b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270725} {:answer/id #uuid "dff6ea96-0ee2-5bc4-80b8-7b9f1617a512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2bd73b-78d1-5e9e-92ad-582b63f4ea0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "308956a9-f5f0-59d1-8748-67ba18adb1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141381" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7bf421-a5a6-51a7-bbde-dcaec6ed4724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269688} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0c5476-651b-5c29-aaf7-9c8b4495a59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270462} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8eb468-d843-5e6f-a576-53b1846a95f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270466} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8ff2c0-77a5-54e0-af66-4eb8ca206ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d9c7cb-8c90-5531-a052-3b459d3163b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bedf2d85-2b35-59e9-bfbf-70576d25817c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141386" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall  the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e35ad90-2f1b-5a63-97f8-d0ee68eca5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269392} {:answer/id #uuid "2fde21b2-4071-56ca-975f-426d92a1cb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269698} {:answer/id #uuid "752357c7-c757-545e-b673-5e6aef138895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270304} {:answer/id #uuid "9758a9f8-4285-59af-9944-c703f3e02a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270598} {:answer/id #uuid "c828b14e-ea43-5244-865e-f22017cdf7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fde21b2-4071-56ca-975f-426d92a1cb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269698} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc92cb1-fc14-5bd0-a868-45afb00eeb79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34dfcc70-f55e-5b7a-a24a-4dbf1116a030" :question/math-academy-id "q-14144" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f9529-efb3-578e-8744-809e1101f04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269712} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3730b5-4247-558a-bff6-c3d95ce3ec3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270236} {:answer/id #uuid "83f3968d-ef3f-53f0-ba78-c6e9d8fb524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270408} {:answer/id #uuid "86ca815b-5e6f-55e6-91c6-bd46a039a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270438} {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca9f1ac-2135-555e-8f76-360ce0db72a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aeb1ad5f-0910-5dea-b64a-e03889cc2cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14145" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{6}-52{x}^{2}+50x-8,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110ee8cd-e830-5bb3-bab0-f16cb26fe2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269421} {:answer/id #uuid "13e31713-81cb-5dec-8652-93d586dd34fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269445} {:answer/id #uuid "29d7d2be-f02c-521d-b752-bf360c856fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269648} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb20cc7-de16-5bcf-8e1b-43826770ca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270109} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee48d03a-569e-51f8-be78-887782d15f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f5b61f-2f3f-5cda-a551-c1e861cdd220" :question/math-academy-id "q-141513" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} {:answer/id #uuid "80e62b24-0b58-586c-ab5e-10a92a99e5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270386} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8982a4-a551-5a56-96cc-144410267cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270506} {:answer/id #uuid "94a242c2-81bc-53a1-99fc-de5cfa4e26aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270571} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa96b06-cbed-595d-86c7-fdb048fe292f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} :answer-field/id #uuid "40dc7a0f-2584-5d4a-b845-07be3a13c2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17fd33d-fef8-5118-8c44-b94f68ba1d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-141527" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+11y+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} {:answer/id #uuid "5c56dfc4-4aef-5331-872d-da2b2274bae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270094} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd458c1-82ff-5e70-925e-2f5ec028580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270924} {:answer/id #uuid "ca96e8bc-27e6-5cce-ac58-a15424f3db5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271049} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d5b9e-029a-5589-8dcb-8d46977be99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ddc0f-2757-59e8-9e14-a04b8fd5611f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ca9c1a-eab9-55fb-98fa-ee3277281e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141536" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+7y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129c0ce9-31d0-501f-9253-202530581425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269432} {:answer/id #uuid "1e413b35-35e1-5ce6-a283-f4ff4b83d4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269522} {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} {:answer/id #uuid "899c0f97-acdd-567e-ab3c-221326e9fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270456} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c19f26-9f49-5230-b707-0daae3af6b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b561ff-d7d5-56bf-bd70-881cdbd1aea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7d3420b-3f28-5142-8868-756f1394ea88" :question/math-academy-id "q-141537" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+10x+16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+16\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400935} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184170 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209d435-198d-515f-b8b4-60c9562f6bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269560} {:answer/id #uuid "273798d