remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c9dab48-f4ec-50d5-997f-66003dcb0d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} {:answer/id #uuid "78463560-21e0-5b25-866c-e90cd7340bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388844} {:answer/id #uuid "b9db4f34-08d3-52b7-b130-1953818d1cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388845} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcc5cac-e58c-525f-92e0-44013ae6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} :answer-field/id #uuid "32deee12-64c9-53b5-a191-c9595d854af8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd3a3fd-0ec4-5982-bdba-cd236ee44004" :question/math-academy-id "q-141811" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}+24x=-11.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+6x+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+24x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+6x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $36$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36+36 & =-11+36 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e24f9c-d383-5b77-a22a-8e142a072f0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33162c16-a26a-585b-b93a-caff202d8543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269736} {:answer/id #uuid "472c723c-83ab-5626-ad0b-8949bf5249de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269915} {:answer/id #uuid "929563b1-603a-55ce-b34f-10a3c1c67754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270550} {:answer/id #uuid "dbafdf59-1e66-5dce-9f48-65bc473487a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271191} {:answer/id #uuid "f1de299d-a3c5-5ebc-80da-bb0051f2d8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "929563b1-603a-55ce-b34f-10a3c1c67754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270550} :answer-field/id #uuid "16c6ad9b-646e-5a06-a5b2-bcae2f52ebb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0de10d9-8ebd-58e0-b040-9c2f5e59c9c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141812" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-8x=20.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-8x+16$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+16=(x-4{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}-16 & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $16$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2}-16+16 & =20+16 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |x-4| & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4-6=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-2{)}^{2}+{10}^{2}=104.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241b1404-c2aa-575d-8065-ec106981f5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269585} {:answer/id #uuid "7919a728-b543-536d-8054-0a184713c2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270341} {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} {:answer/id #uuid "e3105ac8-37d5-5739-abc9-4ef7c2fc18d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271267} {:answer/id #uuid "f25bf37e-9c95-5124-b37f-32e6744d69d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe18474-5ded-5269-8f07-0ba5488b85e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c3581b3-19ec-58f3-879c-94addceac5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141817" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5{y}^{2}-40y=-76.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-8y+16$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+16=(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-40y & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-8y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}) & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-16] & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $80$ to both sides and then dividing by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80+80 & =-76+80 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ |y-4| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c261f4-b523-5e38-b911-79bebe41075e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269321} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e7353a-2dd4-5608-ac70-fe6d8e0291e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270848} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe132bb-0af0-51c8-afe2-8c3302082c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270925} {:answer/id #uuid "bc901ce9-1a00-525b-a2cd-700e1bd17ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270928} {:answer/id #uuid "c301dd7e-cbc1-5a8b-80c7-f2bfe83da7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05c261f4-b523-5e38-b911-79bebe41075e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(21,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269321} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e2b396b-77e2-5134-94df-60067b3ce9c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08773f73-4575-52a7-9948-7d0d1e95d791" :question/math-academy-id "q-14185" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-x+10y+21=0$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x+10y+21 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-x+10(0)+21 & =0 \\\\ -x+21 & =0 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(21,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1cd77c1-d94a-50e2-a2d2-310f30af1efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{13},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395757} {:answer/id #uuid "932f6c0b-7bfc-5138-a5fc-88d5849899ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{13}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395758} {:answer/id #uuid "62a3c355-7a46-5e69-a9b4-100e157d8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395759} {:answer/id #uuid "1acd5f2b-ea97-5c92-bd3f-4c0fa0954dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{13}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395760} {:answer/id #uuid "de725257-e555-5095-bd67-53b03fd19c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{13},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1cd77c1-d94a-50e2-a2d2-310f30af1efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{13},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395757} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb45a2c-578d-5e71-917a-16d23dca9a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a1f70ec-1a84-5f86-99bd-fdd4752087f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14191" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}+13x-15y-2=0$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+13x-15(0)-2 & =0 \\\\ 13x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\frac{2}{13},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "591bd29b-f88e-5bec-aeeb-18b7f495581c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270056} {:answer/id #uuid "6d007451-1012-551a-a1a2-6cd3617e7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270235} {:answer/id #uuid "8924c245-63c5-593a-ac04-35ad4e886a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270451} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dcb6bb-a92a-5a25-976f-4cbc5392b851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271155} {:answer/id #uuid "e526b157-5281-503f-a120-9b2f67b7b748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d007451-1012-551a-a1a2-6cd3617e7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270235} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4abf10-6ff0-5441-9d67-0a97db0614ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81b50d75-05a1-5fec-ae6a-c17be8793e34" :question/math-academy-id "q-141937" :question/problem "![](images/58/583cde62650a6940389d807eee0ffeabdafcbb0da7b8d416af88e3c4d1b7bd71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,0].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downwards like a frown ($⌢$) over the intervals $(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$\n\n![](images/ce/ce57fc818eebd0cc5007e80a54b49d613eeb2823eefc98e946862ff9f7b68e0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "accd213e-a18c-57f9-a2cf-0e3297615ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398468} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8269e6-822d-57d9-a7d5-81b77feea90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398469} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d90684-6641-5156-b37c-32b02b1bdd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398470} {:answer/id #uuid "21d7fa3c-5da8-50fa-8d80-231c8289856a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398471} {:answer/id #uuid "70cad25b-cabf-5869-8148-7ca6a521ce0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8269e6-822d-57d9-a7d5-81b77feea90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398469} :answer-field/id #uuid "412e6109-4d77-5424-95b6-1257add5ac9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b85652aa-105e-56e5-bee4-742b27b9d838" :question/math-academy-id "q-141999" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b237f9e857b1ed87de447aa79c14d359429e71d8afe7ab8eab201eba7ffb6af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B+2D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Therefore, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+2D=2+2\\cdot (1)=2+2=4.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1477d75c-69cc-56f6-83aa-951c0dfbc5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398475} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a7f65b-0eec-54b0-bd1b-1489777d7346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398476} {:answer/id #uuid "251fc1e5-eecc-5524-8fcc-50a9a87bac4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398477} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a9476a-83e1-5900-adda-60bacbc12a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398478} {:answer/id #uuid "823522a9-4039-541d-b6e1-6b18113ea496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a9476a-83e1-5900-adda-60bacbc12a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398478} :answer-field/id #uuid "25043d55-6e38-5a47-9ce8-35564e623dc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "949bd398-6573-53d2-bf2d-21cf0dde3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-142000" :question/problem "![](images/98/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e24f9c-d383-5b77-a22a-8e142a072f0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08658d01-89d0-5f4d-8f01-ca95f49a8f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269345} {:answer/id #uuid "91635a34-3072-5810-bbe9-632fc13648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270534} {:answer/id #uuid "b63daf1b-11dc-55f7-bcdf-962180261257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270887} {:answer/id #uuid "c16cb9a6-c3c8-5125-ae28-f529c2244338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270983} {:answer/id #uuid "f5121dc9-a221-5173-bccc-27c79eacbf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91635a34-3072-5810-bbe9-632fc13648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3ef694-6ed8-54b6-8d68-ea9692dda819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36afe386-dcce-5814-bb16-64bd8f39ae6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142015" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-10x=-21.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-10x+25$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-10x+25=(x-5{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-21 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}-25 & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2}-25+25 & =-21+25 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x-5| & =2 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}={3}^{2}+{7}^{2}=58.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06722ca7-474e-5595-bf36-153b1775fd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269327} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a8511-de08-55eb-a39b-8c1d7fb3526d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269532} {:answer/id #uuid "35238dad-241f-56ad-8d8f-e9b4f0502e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269755} {:answer/id #uuid "e79b8881-98bb-524f-b8e6-1f50afbbbdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271294} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7b137e-47ec-5070-a46e-9c1151db82e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7b137e-47ec-5070-a46e-9c1151db82e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271335} :answer-field/id #uuid "c897889a-b632-5109-a8b5-b76c95145861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ad9fc7-9d0e-5199-bbb4-6a03531ef9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-142026" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1add8174-ecc0-5924-a357-a237227abb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269484} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6527fb-28b3-591d-9a68-37bb2ed8eb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269523} {:answer/id #uuid "4923a1f4-3c21-5dc4-832a-0c5c1f4015d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269936} {:answer/id #uuid "bd09ad0b-d0b2-5249-a22f-07c77761d468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270936} {:answer/id #uuid "c9efdfa7-fd05-551d-98ac-d8d9b12ea4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd09ad0b-d0b2-5249-a22f-07c77761d468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270936} :answer-field/id #uuid "999b90ee-3fc3-56da-b03e-7304992bbc26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bb121bf-9e48-5785-a2df-744a4832e310" :question/math-academy-id "q-142031" :question/problem "![](images/47/47b3d021487b88f0a335fc070bfd4846a0ea54dd137f6a0db165db5b27180d45.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/89/899cb5979c943b1d385f9015ffa57bf97d4a47b10fb2be4e653272ed16494d73.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62401300-96d1-56d5-9ee8-f9c4a7c3bc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270134} {:answer/id #uuid "70626190-f544-5b0c-bdad-970c1bd574c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270263} {:answer/id #uuid "73fd380b-5896-5429-8b75-1a92ef994916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270292} {:answer/id #uuid "beb805c3-9735-51f3-8d7b-56423fc172c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270955} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd55e37-172c-5e0b-8352-3139633aa32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70626190-f544-5b0c-bdad-970c1bd574c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270263} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff788ec-47ea-5d3c-a323-f22a2955dff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a51e757c-6a44-5439-956b-057bca4d2d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-142069" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8dbc36f0b404c1704e9add4b668b257703d6d163f825695b9404a33633da3ea7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](images/7f/7f42ed4bdb45d14497c70b0e2e98af95442d9bf8e6aaa577766dec1c5df2b31f.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave  down ($⌢$) at $x=a$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9ceefc-3aa8-5c13-90a1-e4a084f720cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e4d2de-99a8-5c5a-bcd7-bfbf63902855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1497c291a9a068a414503d3081f52c5df56019d496a3f6c16cc905e6a20b97fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388850} {:answer/id #uuid "a1177d92-f4ff-58b1-ae15-c2d12bba3b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e6af7aceed6e31bccd5f568d50ac1c897b98cc568d383ea559a8b61abc203e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388851} {:answer/id #uuid "a935f1aa-2e46-5432-9942-28834f14200a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1cc25938438d7ef2ad77bd5047a1417e5c0dba609e630bde7c3dee9dffa205ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388852} {:answer/id #uuid "184cb15f-141c-5d51-b648-0f352aa708e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388853} {:answer/id #uuid "98de12f6-db2b-5f19-b507-54a7283acb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9febb7b2ffb18d3d4d1ea5976fb0de6ee164689d01d82cf89dfb83000febffb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "184cb15f-141c-5d51-b648-0f352aa708e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388853} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a641e6-d96d-56ee-a09e-0f810a98dfec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cee14fe-e375-5314-aab5-fe2b10bd1948" :question/math-academy-id "q-142097" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $4x-2y<6?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & <6 \\\\ -2y & <-4x+6 \\\\ 2y & >4x-6 \\\\ y & >2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=2x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>2x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](images/2f/2fe69dace2487c1b6dc73fcd122fd358513d8ac9528b3195d2075f8b62c75b4a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a831251-1892-5348-acb0-08de00fc7410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec99abbe475fc356cfd36415012fe1a6e8e611895452b8e7b0f3f1b8457d61d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388857} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0f6713-300a-5873-adfe-ba515b646bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/801b9576f2c9840eac75479d8983eb8b12a8a1138da7bb361f417c14ffad6823.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388858} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3375a7-8226-5fcc-9260-c5266d4a00a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b68f8b6a6f5b72bbc9c11ca7c1af87965330cc72d5f9700486c80a4d22a08849.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388859} {:answer/id #uuid "bd596374-4a82-557d-9a74-1dcda7c6daf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce1f17aa0fde89c6373bdc4499a1e2bb2c976f41b38d97568f7e73ed8d57dd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388860} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcb4582-ca85-526e-b94c-668fb6055ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a8b3896667e3401eb610625ee71607c221ad7cdaf2d4ccea79bdbe54665ec7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a831251-1892-5348-acb0-08de00fc7410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec99abbe475fc356cfd36415012fe1a6e8e611895452b8e7b0f3f1b8457d61d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388857} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed07d46-b98a-5fa7-9c22-1b406322df54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60ebcac8-bc59-5dde-9394-be5d3c916d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-142112" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x+2y\\le 4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & \\le 4 \\\\ 2y & \\le -x+4 \\\\ y & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**. \n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line. \n\n![](images/8b/8b906cf167528f484109528a93732dc2b8d73558bb02f454bb520af87efde3c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090d142f-f7f9-5a7b-b72e-52a078df1eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269350} {:answer/id #uuid "1d201ccd-85fb-549a-9e9c-531394fc50aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269507} {:answer/id #uuid "76ee6731-902a-5dee-b46b-c4380fed56c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270319} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c049c-a748-56a4-b205-01a20869b559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270893} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5c1f2-a0ab-5100-8db9-3071ee8511c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ee6731-902a-5dee-b46b-c4380fed56c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270319} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8980b78-b788-5d75-b585-9a0b63595d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1de0aea-4bea-5c0c-94bb-b2903c951763" :question/math-academy-id "q-142142" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{210}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef5d4f19-5602-54a9-96ea-4e42054ff5c8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Greater Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d289c8-f2ba-5e7d-8df2-ac922b653604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269322} {:answer/id #uuid "2045997a-c29b-5682-9690-d4803b53e48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 3$ or $x\\ge 6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269539} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc78030-e11b-505d-8b3b-a395364a5a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\le x\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270244} {:answer/id #uuid "87c0387a-3b4b-5646-9b8f-6715f8f992c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<3$ or $x>6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270444} {:answer/id #uuid "9d893273-9034-5596-9c94-9cfc2b550725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge -3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dc78030-e11b-505d-8b3b-a395364a5a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\le x\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270244} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45e81b5-1cf8-5dd9-bed8-268515bde3f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "503c0efb-79c2-51f6-ad5d-0c4262a2faa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142180" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+9x-18\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-18\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+9x-18\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+9x-18 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-9x+18) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=3$ and $x=6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/d4/d4c28d26425c3c5bfc95dece73c8d8cb74f395f2de04a2c2ee1b1fd69f8433ed.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches the $x$-axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $3\\le x\\le 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184333 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbe80595-c807-506c-a356-566f1edb3402" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Less Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b037b9b-e3aa-5d64-9ab7-75143587f57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269655} {:answer/id #uuid "5f361f45-2e24-52d2-8ba1-e2141f5458b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270112} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc53eed-3ccc-5cee-85ea-f34c5a0ca936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270227} {:answer/id #uuid "85d0e095-6d5b-5db1-a55e-d9d582564d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270427} {:answer/id #uuid "8639d9ec-29d0-58a8-b9c8-8ad01cbfd00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bc53eed-3ccc-5cee-85ea-f34c5a0ca936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270227} :answer-field/id #uuid "15997eef-47b6-5fa2-86ac-a18455aef1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "516a42b4-18d7-5e17-814b-bcd4a9e53678" :question/math-academy-id "q-142314" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+x-12\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x-12\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+x-12\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/eda4704129b905a10e379254dfd6d1ff07d593d7f97bbc6126e9d27bfec2f9f2.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-4\\le x\\le 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184333 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbe80595-c807-506c-a356-566f1edb3402" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Less Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb10726b-626d-526b-b3f9-de6bad9f3f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400960} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8c3f4-5711-584d-9263-fed31acf6c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400961} {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400962} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8d0e9c-0806-580a-b031-8a01a725abe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\le x\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400963} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a43c47-c698-531e-a0a3-0b2da98a2630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -5$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2ce6b8-4640-53b8-b17b-3464a0fe587d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f59dad02-8b17-598a-a754-71bfff242018" :question/math-academy-id "q-142315" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+6x-5\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+5) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cfa66b5aa6a3f29e7154e329926a47bbbb0eb45dce3251148986785f09ee383.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b740b5-03e6-52a1-8778-bb3d5a4643d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269793} {:answer/id #uuid "3b972a24-5c70-5eae-9d0c-716d33795b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} {:answer/id #uuid "483860b4-2eb2-5833-8723-a77620ce5853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269925} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8613fe-ae9c-573e-9a33-0b83852831ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270494} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81f94e-33e1-514c-88bb-fa409515889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b972a24-5c70-5eae-9d0c-716d33795b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f6e6d2-08b8-5639-bf32-ba297bae54b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c02ea3-6977-53a9-ae04-f813c6f1d08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142369" :question/problem "![](images/40/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083343 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064f38a9-6565-5259-b73e-38e9a5bf6ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {2}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269324} {:answer/id #uuid "29fc075b-1f90-5ba4-b267-72126324de52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {4}^{+}}({\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269650} {:answer/id #uuid "5e30c8ba-7321-5f29-b158-fcfc42391f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270105} {:answer/id #uuid "d23cf462-84fa-5c02-955c-f8bbd06dc2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271118} {:answer/id #uuid "f23df302-0072-5c0a-9e7f-b2913dca9ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f23df302-0072-5c0a-9e7f-b2913dca9ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271397} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac4203d-ddee-5a21-9768-4c4a3a7f55f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "693a32b1-39ba-5f91-9f8b-21a365f10248" :question/math-academy-id "q-142389" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aad8bda-3338-5a30-9437-cc3702c58311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396061} {:answer/id #uuid "327b2ad5-797c-54cc-89ed-97281f8970bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396062} {:answer/id #uuid "bef389b0-e3ae-5a1a-8b87-cc5f8f7fe4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396063} {:answer/id #uuid "16f5c998-e55d-56ad-a2e1-414b9c779c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396064} {:answer/id #uuid "f30933e4-e411-5b64-94cc-db4bc0952b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "327b2ad5-797c-54cc-89ed-97281f8970bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396062} :answer-field/id #uuid "402f8b77-e682-57ca-9212-0a3e5c1ad08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ecc876e-1e30-5d04-b02b-e0d45341e6f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14241" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b7acbd4f24b6bbd20dd00fc042405324c069d28db0102c2cd7dd49e6b3bbccd.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,4)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-8) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(0-2) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$ and  $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9382dc-fb73-541e-bc41-986c2b0487c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269371} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddd3d31-aad7-54e3-bac9-d739185ba62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269681} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2438bc-9e5f-56ed-9935-8905934dccab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270463} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa6126a-c809-5449-a098-b98507d007d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270522} {:answer/id #uuid "d7074d52-895a-53e7-b616-0fef35af4a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b9382dc-fb73-541e-bc41-986c2b0487c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269371} :answer-field/id #uuid "2736f469-6d71-5845-8e29-ea99b9db88ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d5f1ae2-d446-5455-83a4-5e182e77c947" :question/math-academy-id "q-142436" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7fd7d815bd006c27be4fc793e4cdc8c66ccd119d86167ad7f7cb1da64a4f2bd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd47d40-053d-580f-8ef6-d1b7dea8bc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269823} {:answer/id #uuid "91dfe027-7e34-5fbb-ac22-0eeedb6cc380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270540} {:answer/id #uuid "af17243f-34c3-59ab-bed7-fdbf3855bc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270824} {:answer/id #uuid "c75cf06a-ada1-577c-8303-4d49a0383776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271024} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6d6f40-a766-55c2-b6fc-5cc3ee90b932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd47d40-053d-580f-8ef6-d1b7dea8bc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269823} :answer-field/id #uuid "81440e2a-2d3e-59d0-8785-f957ed0391b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f14aa2fa-d615-53a6-833b-93cabfaae0ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-14248" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cac89f66c8c1e5e0ac6a9857cb7db48d3ca6edc2a83fb2ba3a63b0809cfe6ab0.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162071 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96760d15-5e56-529b-a5da-1697dc782031" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec4b385-93cf-5f29-8025-84628538d63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269403} {:answer/id #uuid "58aed0b9-7c56-5dd2-b027-af04153a5a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270052} {:answer/id #uuid "62e30278-9444-526a-97c9-bb9e40734c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270143} {:answer/id #uuid "7d50103d-8778-576c-8c64-70f15285a779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270367} {:answer/id #uuid "9b822cff-80b7-5953-853d-604fe84d300b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58aed0b9-7c56-5dd2-b027-af04153a5a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270052} :answer-field/id #uuid "338a5c7d-ce42-5c77-be89-5a7c46a6c9e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59735bc3-a414-5af3-be99-af79ebdc3032" :question/math-academy-id "q-142559" :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(Y=1).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=1) & ={f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162071 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96760d15-5e56-529b-a5da-1697dc782031" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b6e470-16bb-591e-82d1-376c55018993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269264} {:answer/id #uuid "1870d620-fa08-5ba5-ba86-ca30448c4b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269473} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdabb3f-b0da-51e1-8095-155d348e97fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269815} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9c777c-6e6d-5fb9-8bd1-0f58d24983c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269971} {:answer/id #uuid "93567cdf-fd92-5b25-a799-e9a0a381b9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b6e470-16bb-591e-82d1-376c55018993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269264} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c225d7-1e3c-5000-845d-48f5b60a7581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24a20428-6f3d-5f93-ade5-d814c51a6012" :question/math-academy-id "q-142562" :question/problem "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(Y=2).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=2) & ={f}_{Y}(2) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(2,2)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =0.25+0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Cumulative Probability Given a Joint Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c37897-823c-5869-93b1-f9389c000327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269899} {:answer/id #uuid "5a85b663-478d-5a24-b1ab-f713bb0147f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270073} {:answer/id #uuid "75badddd-ef33-5dd7-b1d5-49bdc5708b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270308} {:answer/id #uuid "c0debcf0-fc7b-5f78-a577-0537e5df3bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270978} {:answer/id #uuid "db52da11-884b-5df2-8ae3-cc5e9eb27f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75badddd-ef33-5dd7-b1d5-49bdc5708b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270308} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40675b2-41a5-5c75-8425-9a3a29305edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cdcd4af-d654-55b5-9a81-3fe7c8ead4ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-142575" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{4}{7}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<2) & =P(Y\\in {1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4171c5-a734-541a-8466-69b56ce0d873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269359} {:answer/id #uuid "38ecd45c-ba43-5032-b302-2a4db3f68db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269785} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aa3d76-7de4-57d1-9a49-fedf8212ceab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270846} {:answer/id #uuid "b2780b36-6d65-5d14-9b03-a066bbecba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270864} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf951d6-95fc-5d3d-8bf4-6811a7297a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf951d6-95fc-5d3d-8bf4-6811a7297a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270934} :answer-field/id #uuid "c146e3ac-1df2-5b81-bf01-9382213bcac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a8c132a-a85d-5f19-bf08-0d6e90b34efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14267" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{e}^{-y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{-y} & =-{e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ -y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{x}) \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-{e}^{0}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ C-1 & =2 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-{e}^{x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Cumulative Probability Given a Joint Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b199b00-2e32-51b7-a0a2-2af7edc30793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269945} {:answer/id #uuid "a48bdf26-5f1a-5acc-91d7-652daba55fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270736} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7ab526-7b74-5841-a507-644509a22dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270792} {:answer/id #uuid "e233ef36-ef94-5e6c-895a-e363b992f2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271256} {:answer/id #uuid "e984de61-29d8-5c1f-b4c2-570abff33fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e233ef36-ef94-5e6c-895a-e363b992f2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271256} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c1240f6-22a0-5c0f-bdb8-d662a62e2820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d18fe4f-c908-58f7-9a78-04f4bc6d6db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142718" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(|Y|>0).$" :question/worked-solution "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>0) & =P(Y\\in {-1,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=-1)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.2+0.45 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110553 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03491655-9b81-5fc0-bf20-e4f74a9c72ad" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d148a20-6732-5181-8bc1-8502aefbed4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269961} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f6e86-481a-56ab-9316-dbe29ee9a431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270484} {:answer/id #uuid "a1948bb4-ba66-523b-85c0-dc26ae8fc9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270703} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d68be5-ce6f-591b-8232-480ac7934752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270753} {:answer/id #uuid "d82cd36e-e05b-529e-bdd4-c33af7e11490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d82cd36e-e05b-529e-bdd4-c33af7e11490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271167} :answer-field/id #uuid "13271a5c-0f37-525d-add3-244c00c711ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d7d6733-f73e-5b97-aa59-836de3d2a188" :question/math-academy-id "q-142764" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\text{ }\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\text{ }\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\,({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\text{ }\\theta ={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640012b-79f9-5f6b-a26b-a7f4fe8c0e7b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e50294c-f2fb-5493-b386-188a5e5c6454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269396} {:answer/id #uuid "10c68d72-483e-5e6f-bc5d-9a17b61d2b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269418} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a5799-d4bb-5fa0-acc3-0994f9a11a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270507} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bb409-c8b2-5599-90d8-2cde8b83b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270981} {:answer/id #uuid "d3274d62-cd90-5dd9-a3e7-e8835fdd2082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a5799-d4bb-5fa0-acc3-0994f9a11a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270507} :answer-field/id #uuid "b35fc1c6-91c7-5f33-8f19-88ce55beb8ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c559d29c-6df4-58e1-9d14-484afb67e84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142822" :question/problem "${(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & ={sec}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =1+{tan}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =2+{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+{tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac80eed8-d801-5275-9bd1-73ba43fadb38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030f83aa-0bd6-560c-b4a9-81a4e36475d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.45$ | $0.55$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269296} {:answer/id #uuid "104bcea6-f55b-5751-8dc2-5e7e2460d261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269415} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5fd84e-fc28-51a3-9fca-8b6b215042af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270374} {:answer/id #uuid "89b523de-8394-5537-83d0-ee038404d50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270459} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d9546b-6985-505d-8091-e8ab70e2891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030f83aa-0bd